  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Late in a tied game, this team found the moment it needed.",
    "what_this_means": "A one-run game through seven innings doesn't break itself. Ty France delivered the go-ahead sac fly in the eighth, converting a late chance when the Padres had runners in position. But what matters is this: they were in position because they kept competing after falling behind early. They trailed after the first, answered with three runs in the second, matched Toronto's two-run fourth with two of their own in the eighth. The bullpen. Marinaccio clean, Hart and Morejon holding, Mason Miller closing it. Gave them the margin to work with. They scored on three of their RISP opportunities. That's not empty; that's enough.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is what fragile looks like when it wins. They're now two straight, both by one run, both decided late. The formula hasn't broken, but it's running on fumes. Offense waiting until it absolutely must deliver, pitching giving them room to wait, the closer getting the last out before anything unravels. M\u00e1rquez lasted four innings and left early. The bullpen carried the load. When teams survive like this twice in a row, the margin starts feeling like a habit. That's when the math gets dangerous.",
